   Behold, an Unthinkable Present
   The purpose of the individual, of society, and of civilization.
   Nicholas Tran

“The greatest inequality that exists within this world is that between the living and the dead.”


Preface
Our society defends its own limitations by asserting that death is natural, that it is a normal part of our existence.
If death is natural, then rules of nature must be rewritten. 
Our society normalizes poison by preaching moderation, by telling us that a suboptimal life is acceptable as long as your habits are a net positive.
If a slow suicide is the societal norm, then that culture must be eradicated and replaced. 
I refuse to accept a world in which death is seen as normal. I intend to live in one where it is seen as an outlier, one in which life is seen as the most precious thing on this planet.

Life is the prerequisite to everything.
The prerequisite to love, to happiness, to satisfaction, and pleasure. It is the prerequisite to all good, the prerequisite to human advancement, exploration, and more importantly, a prerequisite to the fulfillment of the human imagination and potential. Therefore, the purpose of life is life itself. The optimization of life is necessary for the infinite continuation of our existence.

Everything is fine in moderation assuming that there is no alternative to poison, except there is. Yet, that is a problem that is constrained by accessibility, logistics, and profitability. You can reduce the potency, but the technology to fully erase it is not yet there.

If you were to give the entire world population the option to kill themselves, what proportion would opt to do it? Of those who would opt to do it, what would be the most common reason? We can only assume that those who would choose to are in a state of depression. But it is not a problem concerning the world, it is a problem concerning the individual…right? There is something wrong with them, they are not right in the head. At least that is what we tell ourselves to justify our lack of agency. It is easier to change the individual than it is to change the world that has created that individual in the first place. Now what would you do if such a world was really that horrible and that their reasons for suicide were valid? Would we encourage them to do it? Or would we encourage them to live on? When you witness someone on the brink of suicide, the natural instinct is to prevent them from doing so no matter how horrible their situation is. We tell them to focus on the positive aspects, to change their mindset, to distract themselves. We give them hope, we want them to see a brighter future. But all individuals are different, their situations are different. Could you say the same to an 80 year old who lives most of her remaining life in pain? What “brighter” future is there for such a person? Now what if they were 20? Yes. But what do you say to a 20 year old that was diagnosed with a severe illness that left them dismembered and their only childhood memory is being bullied for their deformities?

What levers would the world have to pull to get you to the threshold where suicide is the only option?
Do you have a family? No, do you have a family that loves you? 
Can you realistically achieve what your heart desires with the current body that you possess? 
Do you have friends? No, do you have friends who you trust? 
Is eating enjoyable to you? 
Does it hurt to breathe? 
Does it hurt to swallow? 
Does it hurt to move? 
Do you feel a sharp pain in your heart, as if you are being stabbed every time you think about something that upsets you? 
Has the love of your life ever left you for someone else? 
Have you ever felt like society does not value you? 
Do you feel weak? 
Every day when you wake up, do you feel as if you are 300 meters deep in the ocean and the water pressure is slowly crushing you? 
Does it feel like there are tiny pebbles rubbing against your brain every time you move your head? 

Many do not ever reach the threshold where suicide is the only option, however; a much larger proportion has reached the threshold for a passive suicide. The result is risky self destructive behavior. Why aim for more when there is virtually no time left? Why care about the world when you won’t be there to see it collapse? Why care about what happens to your health when you do not have much to live for? It is a loss of hope. How would you feel if your parents or your spouse erased your existence along with all the memories they made with you in their final years of life?

Could you attain whatever you wanted in life if you were given an extra 500 years? How much would your life change if your IQ was increased by an extra 15 points? How much of your life would change if your body was in peak physical condition throughout your entire life.

Healthspan is the lever that increases potential, achievement, and in a way, it offers a second chance, and if immortality is achieved then it offers the opportunity of infinite chances. What I see in this society is the gradual self destruction of the individual in the midst of rapid civilizational advancement.

Proof
Murder is the designated symbol of evil. Murder in its core, a loss of life. Our moral intuition is a poor guide in regards to humanity’s greater goals. The world of public health offers a better alternative: Years of Life Lost (YLL), which calculates how much potential life was taken. 

There are roughly 20,000 homicides per year in the United States with an average of 47 potential years lost per victim which yields to ~940,000 YLL annually. Deaths as a result of obesity occur roughly 300,000 times per year at an average of 15 years lost per victim which yields ~4.5 million annually. From a utilitarian perspective, obesity is roughly 5 times more lethal than homicides. Therefore, if given the choice, I would rather eliminate all obesity than eliminate all murder. Yet, obesity isn’t the only problem that stems from poor health. The truth is, this is a much more dangerous and much more consequential problem than it seems on the surface. In World War II, the YLL over 4 years for the U.S. was 20.35 million. If we expand our equation to include every preventable lifestyle driven condition such as hypertension, atherosis, dementia, etc, the amount is roughly equal to 22.5 million YLL per year. We are essentially fighting and losing World War II on an annual basis. The world condemns murder yet remains neutral and even praises that which leads to death. We criminalize the man with the gun yet subsidize an industry which engineers addictive poisons that are consumed by hundreds of millions of people. The world is blinded by its own moral intuitions. It is illogical to focus on the immediate problems and ignore the underlying causes that have caused those problems in the first place. But that is understandable, humans were not meant to see the underlying issues and the long term effects because we were optimized for short term survival. As such, the primitive human brain must be rewired to fit the modern world and free itself from this evolutionary mismatch. 

What Industry Misses
I witness a scene that feels almost dystopian. I see the booths of companies that claim to reverse aging yet what lies in front are cans of soda, glazed donuts, and danish pastries. Even in a longevity conference you are being served that which is the antithesis of longevity. The longevity space has become so focused on the cure that it has strayed away from the fundamentals. We are betting on things that may or may not work while neglecting what we know for sure works. The diet, the exercise, the daily habits that compound over time to slow the speed of aging. We are passively waiting for these technologies to save us instead of taking matters into our own hands. We focus on the cure and yet neglect the accumulation of decades of debt and the decades of habits that have led to accelerated aging. If you neglect the foundations then you will most likely die before you even get access to the cure and your chances of reaching longevity escape velocity will be significantly reduced.

How much increase in our collective intelligence are we leaving off the table by not prioritizing omega-3 consumption in children? Although an increase in intellect is not that we should see, but rather what it leads to and what it means. Resulting in an inefficient workforce, a reduction in economic output, a reduction in new inventions and life saving interventions. But why should you care? How does that serve to benefit you? Imagine there is a live saving drug or cure, yet it was invented 3 years after you or a loved one died. The person who invented it was raised with suboptimal nutrition, and as a result they developed mild adhd and a reduction in a few IQ points. They were very close to their highest potential, close enough that they were even able to invent that drug in the first place. Except being close is not enough if it means you or your loved one has already died. Had they been raised optimally, they could have developed that cure much earlier and prevented such a tragedy.

People want to extend their healthspan but they do not want to learn the scientific basis of aging. They want to have better health but they don’t want it to interfere with their daily life. Health education has to be so simplified that even a 12 year old could understand. We should not be telling people “you should not consume x because it leads to endocrine disruption”, we should say “you should not consume x because if you do then you may never be able to ever have kids and even if you do manage to have kids, they will be at risk of brain defects, their growth will be hindered, and they will struggle with weight gain over the course of their life.” The cellular mechanisms do not matter to people, only its practical implications do. Health education has to be reshaped in a way that aligns with the individual’s greater goals, because that, in its pure essence, is what health is about: the foundation of all human ambitions. It is a means to an end except in this case, the means is dramatically more important than the end itself.

Death and its Consequences
Have you ever witnessed an elderly person desperately holding on to your hand as they slowly drift away from consciousness? Death is horrible. But I don’t want you to think about it, I want you to feel it.


Behold, an Unthinkable Present
How good must it feel to be surrounded by your loved ones? To be out in nature knowing that there is not a single thing you have to worry about. To see your kids full of energy and thriving knowing that they have a bright future ahead of them. To hold on to the hand of your spouse, to look into the eyes and see them full of love and happiness. To be able to cry tears of joy even if it is just for a moment? What does that actually mean to you? And what extent are you willing to go in order to achieve such a future and extend such a moment?

Behold, an Unthinkable Present
I stared into the corpse of my uncle. The person who used to babysit and play with me when I was a toddler. The person who was always kind and who always volunteered to help others. The person that gave my parents a place to stay when they had no money. I watched my parents praying and kneeling over his grave. I watched as his kids mourned over his body. Such is the price of technological inadequacy. Such is the price of a culture dominated by primitive values.

Behold, an Unthinkable Present
Your mother was terminally ill in a hospital bed. Polyester sheets, clothing, being served processed food in hot plastic containers in a hospital room with no sunlight. Being cared for by nurses who are overworked and sleep deprived. But you did not have enough money to pay for those bills. Doing so would put your family in debt. Therefore, the system has determined that she is no longer worth saving because it is no longer profitable. What incentive is there to make them think you an exception?

Behold, an Unthinkable Present
You see an elderly person working at Walmart. Perhaps you have seen them homeless, perhaps you have seen them eating alone out of a microwaved plastic container. Every time I see that, it makes me sad. I put myself in their shoes and the entire world seems hopeless. What events have happened that have led to that? There is no hope for a better situation, there is no life ahead of them, there is no time left. They do not have any family. Yet they do not want to die, but they also do not want to live. The result is a slow suicide. That number will only begin to increase as a result of the aging population crisis. Over 80% of people on their deathbed have had a significant regret. In the end, regret outweighs gratitude.

Behold, an Unthinkable Present
All of your loved ones have passed away one by one as you watched helplessly. They exist in your memory but pretty soon that will disappear and there will be nothing left. We humans cannot comprehend nothingness so we desperately try to imagine an afterlife where our grievances are resolved. We imagine that those who have passed away are in a better place when deep down in our unconscious lies the possibility that they are not.

Access and Democratization
The aggregated cost of implementing all biotech interventions would amount to millions of dollars per year. Meaning that even in the future, the pursuit of immortality will still be limited to the ultra wealthy. Even if you manage to implement every possible biotech intervention, there is no clear path for what one should do in order to achieve longevity escape velocity.

The pursuit of life must be ideological, and for it to be ideological on a civilizational scale, it must be democratized. It must be a global movement and a fundamental restructuring in the way humans live and in the way humans perceive life.

Background
In the past was a world of scarcity and a world without power. My parents were among the Vietnamese Boat people. My dad was a Vietnam war veteran and served as a paratrooper. My parents witnessed a newly formed communist regime, where the system oppressed you if you lived in the south. Where your children had zero opportunities for social mobility if you fought for the south. All children of the south were forbidden to attend university and were limited to low-paying heavy manual labor. Their bank accounts were frozen and all wealth was stripped. My grandmother was diagnosed with a disease but was denied the cure because she was on the losing side of the war, she died as a result. My dad was captured and sent to a forced labor re-education camp where he had to survive by eating the rats and frogs that he scavenged. Many of his friends died from starvation, disease, and abuse. Following his release, my parents and siblings immediately boarded a small boat where people were packed together like sardines. My parents risked everything and crossed the sea at night to reach Malaysia so that they could eventually come to the U.S. even despite the risk of getting raped, robbed, and butchered by pirates. They did it despite the risk of that small wooden boat capsizing in the middle of a storm, where they had no idea of the direction the boat was headed, everything in their view was pitch black, and every wave reached up to 8-13 feet. When an accelerated death and existential decay is inevitable and when all options are exhausted, life is the last thing left to be gambled. That was the only logical choice, the choice to secure a future for me and my siblings. 

19 years following the birth of my youngest sister, I was born. By accident. I was raised in a household where resources were scarce. Our apartment sink was constantly flooded with sludge. I witnessed my elderly father having to wake up at 3am to fix it in order to prevent the apartment from flooding because the landlords did not want to do anything about it. In our neighborhood, people constantly broke into cars and stole the wheels; my parents had to put on a steering wheel lock to prevent their car from getting stolen, yet it was stolen anyway despite those measures. Inadequate nutrition was common because survival was the priority. Despite all that, this was considered a significant improvement. 

Growing up in school, I witnessed a gradual self destruction. Where children were being served burgers in a hot plastic bag. Where children were being served processed nacho cheese in a hot plastic cup. Where nanoplastics and microplastics entered the body at a young age, causing oxidative stress, hindering neurodevelopment and brain health, and thereby compromising the immune system. It stays in their body forever, up until their death because the culture does not value life. 

I have witnessed a culture of passive suicide. A culture where children praise others for the breaking of rules rather than the progression towards its reform. A culture where the youth engage in toxic self-destructive and risky behavior because society did not give them a purpose. Because their values are the same values of entertainers and not those actively working to advance humanity. Instead of doing whatever it takes to advance humanity forward, I witness a culture that incentives prestige and status. I witness a culture with aimless values, I witness a culture that no longer prioritizes how the children of our future are raised.

I lay down all the events that have led me to my current beliefs. I do not intend to give up my entire life and live the rest of my days working a job that means nothing to me because I witness a world where degeneracy is allowed to fester at the expense of human advancement.

Who am I?
I have envisioned many probable futures. I have seen the chronic psychological stress that resides within my family as a result of the unchangeable events in the past. I have imagined every combination of my family’s deaths and every timeline in which those order of deaths reside. What legacy did they leave behind? What were their biggest regrets? What were their hopes and dreams that have never been fulfilled? I have envisioned a plausible future where someone I loved willingly decided to drive into an intersection. Where their body is split in half and their remains splattered over the glass of a broken window. What will become of the people who always loved them if they were to encounter such a sight? How will their lives change as a result? That scar will run deep. That family will never be the same again because one of their main reasons for living has just been wiped away. They will go down a path of slow suicide, desperately hanging on to the bit of faith that things will be better in the afterlife while neglecting their own life in the current reality.

Over time my world view has shifted from survival of the “good enough” to will of the fittest. I use uncoated 18/10 stainless steel pans to avoid PFAs, I buy mainly 100% organic GOTs certified cotton to avoid toxic residues and microplastics. I drink mainly reverse osmosis water. I don’t smoke, I don’t drink, and I never will. I have eliminated all consumption of high sugar fruits to suppress advanced glycation end products (AGEs) as much as possible while maximizing nutrient density. I exercise 3-4 times a week while maintaining a slight caloric deficit. I wear a mask every time I walk near a busy street to avoid inhaling car pollutants. I take a shot of high polyphenol (1000mg+) extra virgin olive oil daily with meals and consume a handful of almonds, walnuts, and macadamia nuts everyday.  I do squats after every meal to reduce blood sugar spikes. I consume fermented foods regularly to protect against chronic inflammation. If it was scientifically validated, I would do blood transfusions continuously in order to keep my parents alive. I have made it my duty to ensure my parents get enough protein to prevent frailty from old age and thereby extend their lifespan. My mom has gotten teflon flu multiple times so I completely removed all teflon pans and replaced them with stainless steel cookware. I have ensured that all possible uses of plastic have been eliminated and replaced. I have completely replaced all seed oils and ensured no one cooks at very high heats. I have convinced them to get an air filter to prevent the toxic fumes from the gas stove and microplastics circulation in the air. I have replaced the laundry detergent and the dishwashing soap to reduce the risk of toxic residues and carcinogens. In a complacent society, that is seen as OCD and fear mongering. However, I see it as the most logical choice in a world full of potential wrong choices and misaligned incentives which have resulted in a polluted world.

I believe all humans are intrinsically motivated. Even the act of altruism itself is done to fulfill a personal desire. We cannot rely on the goodwill of humanity to drive ourselves forward so we must align incentives in a way that leads to our collective benefit. Because even if I do achieve longevity escape velocity, even if I am on the correct path to immortality. The only thing that stands in the way is human nature. No matter how much society advances, it will be bottlenecked by human nature itself. My philosophy is as follows. Every person has something that drives them. Those desires and goals are determined by evolution and genetics. Whether something is “right” or “wrong” is dependent on whether it serves those individual goals. Everything in my life is an analytical optimization problem. There are different variables, and different inputs to each variable. What combination of values in this input would result in the desired output (the objective)? If I want to live for as long as possible then what combination of foods and what actions at what specific time will allow for that? My worldview is fundamentally instrumentalist. I see anything, physical, or abstract as potential means to an end. Societal norms were meant to be rewritten as civilization becomes more and more advanced. Rules and laws are an illusion in this world, what lies beneath are action and consequence. I believe in the inverse of Murphy's law. Anything that can work, will work. I do not care how improbable something is, if it is possible then it will be done, given enough resources and enough time.

I wish to achieve longevity escape velocity, or in other words, I wish to be immortal. I see my current existence as a gateway to immortality. Either I manage to reach the gate or I don’t. One side lies an eternity of prosperity while the other is an eternity of nothingness, there is virtually nothing in between as the cost is infinite and the potential reward is also infinite.

I have only three objectives in this current existence. It is to:
Exist for as long as possible
Continue the existence of those closest to me.
Ensure humanity continues to exist for eternity.

The sacrifices of my parents have allowed me the opportunity to attend one of the most prestigious schools in the world and the opportunity to pursue any career I wish. Although I am grateful for where I am now, it is not nearly enough; it is only a means to an end. Because working a regular job will not alter my family’s future in any significant way. My parents will die in about 15 years. My sisters will die in about 40 years. With a software engineering salary, I can extend that by about 3-4 years, but in the end it will not matter. They will all die and so will I. 

I wish to buy a home that is scientifically proven to extend healthspan. I want an environment that eliminates stressors and ensures that my mental state will not interfere with my goal of immortality. I would have a team of nutritionists and doctors curate the perfect diet and routine for longevity based on my genetics and current health. I want to ensure that every single possible aspect of my health is monitored at all times so that the future can be predicted and altered. The technologies that Ikor creates will be at the center of this. I want an algorithm to predict the optimal thing to do at any moment in time so that I can both achieve as much as possible while reducing my risk of death by as much as possible. I want to create my own farm, have cows and chickens that are given the optimal foods and are in the best mental state possible. I want to have a team of scientists genetically engineer my fruits and vegetables in order to optimize for longevity. I want to ensure that all parts of my estate are entirely free from toxins and have access to the highest quality air possible. I wish to see a world where all the practical uses of time dilation and Einstein's theory of relativity are applied. I wish to experience the sunset on an exoplanet. I wish to go on a vacation to an alien world. I wish to be able to live long enough to learn all the mysteries of the universe, to explore the entirety of this galaxy, and experience all there is to experience in this world. 

My approach to decisions follows an equation. Estimated chance of success multiplied by reward. Let's say the chances of my success is 0.01% but if I am successful then me and my family get to live for an eternity. I would do anything to give them even a 0.01% chance at immortality because 0.01% times infinity is still infinity and if it is not infinity then it is an abnormally large number and if it is not an abnormally large number, then it is a number large enough to outweigh the time invested. 

This startup is not merely the best option I have. It is the ONLY option I have if immortality is the objective. Because every other option will result in my eventual death and the guaranteed death of my family. As long as I have a place to stay and enough for food, all the money will be reinvested into this company. My existence as well as the existence of all those close to me is dependent on its success.

The System We Intend to Create
How many funerals must I attend before this optimization problem is solved? We are entitled to our choices, yet our choices are made based on a lack of education and a lack of understanding of our personal biology. Our cognition is limited. Even if we had all the data in the world, we would not know how to connect it all for any pragmatic use. Therefore, we cannot be trusted to make our own choices. Luckily, our recent technological advances have already determined that we do not have to. Rewriting our nature requires time, time in which most of us do not have. The technology that allows for true immortality does not yet exist. However, the technologies that allow us a glimpse into our biology are either already here or are on the frontier. It is only natural that we utilize it to exploit our nature to the fullest.

Imagine all the possible timelines that branch out from your current present. Every day you are given the option to die as many times as you want. Each time you die, you go back in time in order to and live through each one of these alternative timelines. When you live through a timeline that you see as “optimal” that you wish you keep, you proceed onto the next day and you are unable to go back. This continues until your death. However, if you were to take the optimal course of action to extend your life then you would be allowed more of these days, thereby giving you an opportunity to rewrite your wrongs rather than going back into the past. Except in the real world, on top of not being able to travel back into the past, you also cannot kill yourself to restart your day. You must follow through what you think is the optimal course of action. Every day you only get one try. This is, at its core, the ideal that Ikor strives to achieve. We strive to build an autonomous system that gives you the blueprint for the optimal course of actions based on as much data as possible in order to help you progress towards your personal goals without interfering with your preferences.

Humans are emotional creatures. Even when we logically know what actions need to be done, we still cave in to our evolutionary desires. Therefore, the main question of our design is how would you engineer a system that eliminates that? How would you engineer a system that primes you to make the optimal choices in pursuit of your greater goals? The pursuit of an extended healthspan is not a single medical intervention, but rather it is the infrastructure that continuously intervenes throughout the course of a lifetime. Everything you ought to do in your life requires good health. It is impossible to achieve your highest potential without being in optimal health. Therefore, the goal of health optimization is linked to every single thing that humans can possibly conceive of.

The Vision
Most of us will likely die by age 80. As the current reality stands, we are nothing in this universe. I wish for every person and the collective of humanity to become something. To become significant enough in the vast expanse of this observable universe. I wish to accelerate the advancement of humanity because I see each passing moment of stagnation is an existential risk to our world and to my personal existence. It is a civilizational obligation. I will do so by extending healthspan as much as possible so that the greatest minds of our world are able to contribute as much as they can to scientific advancement. I intend to revamp the education system such that every single person is optimized to achieve their greatest potential and given the opportunity to contribute to our scientific advancement. Most of the top scientists, engineers, and researchers die more than a decade early due to preventable causes and I want to prevent that loss of knowledge. I want to make it mandatory for every research and education institution to have the best dining halls and best foods possible for optimal cognitive performance. I dream of a world free of crime. I believe that all crime must be eradicated in order for a society to thrive to its fullest. I dream of a world where the global policy is optimized for every individual to contribute as much as they can to humanity while also being as happy and fulfilled as possible. I wish to establish a global culture that minimizes the risk of self destruction while maximizing the advancement of humanity. I wish to see ourselves become a type V civilization on the Karsashev scale, to see humanity become the most dominant civilization in the galaxy and in the observable universe. Humanity should never be allowed the chance to go extinct before we ever become something more. 

I am aware that my vision of the future is overly idealistic and outright unrealistic. It will not be possible to achieve in one lifetime but I do not intend to live for one typical lifetime, and I most certainly will not accept anything else.

Addendum
I believe greed, corruption, and complacency must be eradicated. But I do not hate humanity for its flaws because I do not see humans as human. I see ourselves as entities that act according to what millions of years of evolution has programmed us to do. It is not our fault that our primitive instincts have dictated our actions and led to our modern failures. Yet, even if it is not our fault, it is a civilizational obligation to eliminate it as soon as possible. We were not made to rot on this planet, we were made to dominate this galaxy because the forces of nature and the laws of physics have given us this extraordinarily rare opportunity.

Technology, science, and intellect is the only mechanism capable of delivering this reality. On a fundamental level, we only have two objectives. To change our inner world to fit that of the external world, and to change the external world to fit that of our internal world. It is possible to simultaneously do both. In the future, advanced simulations may allow us to simulate the optimal life, to live through any reality we dream of. I love milkshakes, pho, soup dumplings, and fried chicken. But I cannot consume those foods because it is suboptimal for my goal of immortality. But if technology allows us to live forever regardless of what we consume or a simulation and simulates the act of eating fried chicken or the taste of it then that solves that problem entirely.

I will chase immortality for the chance at a “perfect life” because I know my existence is the prerequisite to everything, to every possible experience there is to experience in this world. I do not know what happens after death, but I can ensure it is no better than the “perfect” life that we can create through the use of technology and science in our current reality.

“I don’t want to live forever. Life would be too boring.”
“Just enjoy yourself once in a while, it won't affect you, you’re missing out.”
“My grandpa lived to age 80 and he smoked everyday.”
“At least it’s better than what it used to be.”
“Human nature will never change.”
“It is what it is.”
“Might as well, we will all die anyways.”
Every time I hear these statements it makes me irrationally angry. It is a result of a lack of imagination, a lack in the defiance of social norms, and a lack of faith in the limits to what humanity can achieve. It is the chant of a society that accepts an existential decay. This type of defeatist culture must never be allowed to fester in our transhumanist future. 

Behold, an Unthinkable Present
It is 1986, Vasily Ignatenko and his wife were newlyweds. Following the events of the Chernobyl disaster, Vasily was exposed to massive amounts of radiation trying to put out the fire in Reactor No. 4. Two weeks later, his entire body was an open wound that turned from blue to a dark brown. He coughed up pieces of his internal organs and lungs, his face was swollen and his eyes were fused shut from the swelling. His flesh was falling off of his body even at the slightest touch, so much so that parts of his bones were left visible. Lyudmila, his wife, defied the rules of the hospital despite knowing that she would be exposed to large amounts of radiation and bribed nurses just so she could stay with Vasily and hold his hand. In her monologues, Lyudmila describes how Vasily would vividly plan their life after he was released, he would talk about their upcoming plans, telling Lyudmila how they would go back to his parents' village to plant potatoes, or how they would raise their child. Every time she entered the room he whispered, “You’re here, my little star,” to try and comfort her. He would hide candy under his pillow to surprise her. Every day Lyudmila would sleep on the tiny chair by his bed and refused to leave his side to eat despite the nurses telling her that he was no longer a person but a highly radioactive object. Right before he died he called out to her, repeating her name, “Lyuda! Lyuda!”.

Life is so much more valuable than anything we can ever imagine, more than anything we can even comprehend. 
Life is an anomaly in this universe. Please do not let it go to waste. 
For the sake of humanity, and for the sake of all the possible futures we have yet to experience.

“Do not go gentle into that good night.
Rage, rage against the dying of the light.”


